%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}{Estimation of heterogeneous treatment effects}


\begin{itemize}
\item Beyond questions about the ATE, we may have additional questions when running an experiment
\begin{itemize}
\item Are specific subgroups, such as the most vulnerable subjects, affected differently by the intervention?
\item Is there a subgroup that is particularly helped by the intervention?
\item Is there a subgroup that is \textit{harmed} by the intervention?
\item If we have a limited budget, how can we allocate treatment to maximize outcomes?
\end{itemize}
\item We may know what subgroups we are interested in ex-ante.
\item Or we may want to use data-driven methods to discover the subgroups most affected by our intervention.
\end{itemize}

\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item xxxx
\end{itemize}

}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}{When subgroups are pre-determined}

\begin{itemize}
\item If you already know your subgroups, for example, students who are classified as female vs. otherwise
\item And you just want to test a simple hypothesis of the form
\begin{align*}
H_0 & : &  \tau(x) = \tau(x')\\
H_1 & : &  \tau(x) \neq \tau(x')
\end{align*}

\item You can compare simple differences in subgroup treatment effects.
\begin{align*}
\left( \E[Y_i(1) - Y_i(0) | X_i = x]\right)- \\
\left( \E[Y_i(1) - Y_i(0) | X_i = x']\right)
\end{align*}
\end{itemize}

\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item xxxx
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}{When subgroups are pre-determined}



\begin{itemize}
\item With random assignment, we can just compare differences in differences in means across the two groups
\begin{align*}
\left( \E[Y_i^{obs} | W_i = 1, X_i = x] - \E[Y_i^{obs} | W_i = 0, X_i = x]\right)- \\
\left( \E[Y_i^{obs} | W_i = 1, X_i = x'] - \E[Y_i^{obs} | W_i = 0, X_i = x']\right)
\end{align*}

\item Where for each subgroup, you can estimate as the difference in average outcomes among individuals under treatment vs. control.
\[
\hat \tau_x  = \bar Y^{obs}_{1, x} - \bar Y^{obs}_{0, x}
\]

\end{itemize}

\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item xxxx
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}{When subgroups are pre-determined}



\begin{itemize}
\item You can also use a linear model; this will return the same estimate
\begin{align*}
\hat Y_i = \hat \beta_0 + \hat \beta_1 W_i + \hat \beta_2 W_i\times (X_i=x) + \hat \beta_3 (X_i=x)
\end{align*}
\end{itemize}

\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item xxxx
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%

\begin{frame}{A joint test of treatment-effect heterogeneity}
For $K$ prespecified, mutually exclusive subgroups, fit:
\[
Y_i = \alpha + \tau W_i
  + \sum_{k=1}^{K-1}\delta_k G_{ik}
  + \sum_{k=1}^{K-1}\gamma_k W_i G_{ik} + \varepsilon_i.
\]
\begin{itemize}
\item $G_{ik}$ indicates subgroup membership; omit one reference group.
\item Test the interaction coefficients jointly:
\[
H_0:\gamma_1=\cdots=\gamma_{K-1}=0.
\]
\item Use a robust Wald/$F$-test of these interactions, with covariance estimation appropriate to the assignment design.
\item Rejection is evidence that some subgroup treatment effects differ. Identifying which ones requires follow-up comparisons and attention to multiple testing.
\end{itemize}
\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item xxxx
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}{When subgroups are not pre-determined}

\begin{itemize}
\item You might be interested instead in answering the question, "Is there any treatment effect heterogeneity?"  or
\item \text{Which} group benefits the most from the program?
\end{itemize}

\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item xxxx
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}%{Frametitle}

\begin{itemize}
\item For a broader range of questions, we might want to be able to model
\[
\tau(x) = \E [Y_i(1) - Y_i(0) | X = x]
\]

\item If all covariates are discrete and there are few of them, we can fit a fully interacted regression model, as before.
\item But when there are a lot of covariates and/or they are continuous, we may want to have a more flexible estimation method.
\item Machine learning methods can be helpful to address data-driven problems.
\end{itemize}

\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item xxxx
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%


\begin{frame}{Machine learning}

What is it?
\begin{itemize}
\item A body of \textit{algorithmic} methods \dots  (an algorithm is just a recipe)
\item Somehow part of \textit{artificial intelligence}\dots  (basically, how computers perform tasks)
\item In general, a flexible, \textit{data-driven} approach to make predictions, classify data, or take decisions.
\end{itemize}

\nocite{athey2019machine}

\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item xxxx
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}{Machine learning}

How do the objectives of machine learning differ from those of conventional quantitative methods in the social sciences?
\begin{itemize}
\item In conventional quantitative methods:
\begin{itemize}
\item identify and estimate a target estimand, which is often a parameter in a statistical model,  defined over some specified population of interest.
\item descriptive or causal, e.g., population employment rate, or effect of a policy
\end{itemize}
\item In ML:
\begin{itemize}
\item development of algorithms to make classifications or predictions.
\item e.g., is this a picture of banana or a cat?  Will this person be more likely to click on an ad for sneakers or cookware?
\end{itemize}
\item Is there overlap between the two?
\end{itemize}

\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item xxxx
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}{Model fit vs. prediction}


\begin{itemize}
\item In linear regression, propose a model:
$$ \hat{Y}_i = \hat{\beta}_0 +  \hat{\beta}_1 X_{1i} + \hat{\beta}_2 X_{2i} + \dots + \hat{\beta}_K X_{Ki} $$
\item Select $\hat \beta_0 \dots \hat\beta_K$ to minimize
$$
\sum_{i = 1}^N \hat{\varepsilon_i}^2 = \sum_{i = 1}^N\left(\hat{Y}_i - Y_i\right)^2
$$
\end{itemize}

\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item xxxx
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}{Model fit vs. prediction}


\begin{itemize}
\item For prediction tasks, we could use the same model,
$$ \hat{Y}_i = \hat{\beta}_0 +  \hat{\beta}_1 X_{1i} + \hat{\beta}_2 X_{2i} + \dots + \hat{\beta}_K X_{Ki} $$
\item But select $\hat \beta_0 \dots \hat\beta_K$ to minimize squared prediction error for the next observation:
$$
\hat{\varepsilon}_{N+1}^2 = \left(\hat{Y}_{N + 1} - Y_{N +1}\right)^2
$$
\item Are these the same thing?  (no)
\item If prediction is our goal, can we do better than least squares regression?  (yes)
\end{itemize}

\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item xxxx
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}{Some ML tools}

\begin{itemize}
\item A major concern of ML: \textit{overfit}
\begin{itemize}
\item If your model fits the data \textit{too} perfectly, it's not useful for prediction
\end{itemize}
\end{itemize}

\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item xxxx
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

