%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}{Policy learning}

\begin{itemize}
\item Given information about an individual (age, location, education, etc.) which among multiple treatments should they be assigned?
\item How do you learn a \textit{policy} that will help you determine how to allocate treatment?  \dots potentially accounting for different costs of treatment.
\end{itemize}

\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item xxxx
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}{Matrix completion: the Netflix problem}

\begin{itemize}
\item Netflix has data on viewers, their characteristics, and how they rate movies.
\item The question: how to best recommend to them movies that they have not yet rated?
\item The challenge: come up with the best recommendation algorithm, winner gets \$1 million.
\item This can be framed as a matrix completion problem: put users on rows, movies on columns, predict all of the missing rankings.
\end{itemize}

\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item xxxx
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}{Causal inference: no free lunch}


\begin{itemize}
\item Machine learning does not solve the fundamental problem of causal inference.
\item Causal interpretations are based on assumptions about the data generating process, or knowledge of assignment procedures. These are outside the realm of machine learning methods.
\end{itemize}

\end{frame}


%%%%%NOTE%%%%%
\note{
\scriptsize \singlespacing

\begin{itemize}
\item xxxx
\end{itemize}
\\~\
}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------------%
\begin{frame}{Prediction error}

\begin{itemize}
\item ML methods may do a good job of producing estimates, but how do we account for inference?
\item Cross-validation
\item Bootstrapping
\end{itemize}

\end{frame}


